    },
    {
      "id": "page-5-s0007",
      "page": 5,
      "source_regions": [
        "page-5-r0006"
      ],
      "text": "A flashlight bulb draws 300 mA from its 1.5 V battery. What’s the resistance of the bulb? If the battery degrades and only produces 1.2 V how would the current change?",
      "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
      "complexity": "simple",
      "detailed_figure_description": null,
      "figure_alternative": "Drawing of a lit flashlight with a bulb and two batteries inside its casing.",
      "id": "page-5-s0008",
      "page": 5,
      "source_regions": [
        "page-5-r0008"
      ],
      "type": "figure"
    },
    {
      "id": "page-5-s0009",
      "normalized_math": "I = V/R \\;\\to\\; R = V/I = (1.5\\,\\mathrm{V})/(0.30\\,\\mathrm{A}) = 5.0\\,\\Omega",
      "page": 5,
      "source_regions": [
        "page-5-r0009"
      ],
      "spoken_math_alternative": "Current equals voltage divided by resistance. Therefore, resistance equals voltage divided by current, which equals 1.5 volts divided by 0.30 amperes, or 5.0 ohms.",
      "type": "formula"
    },
    {
      "id": "page-5-s0010",
      "normalized_math": "V \\to 1.2\\,\\mathrm{V}:\\quad I = (1.2\\,\\mathrm{V})/(5.0\\,\\Omega) = 0.24\\,\\mathrm{A} = 240\\,\\mathrm{mA}",
      "page": 5,
      "source_regions": [
        "page-5-r0010"
      ],
      "spoken_math_alternative": "When the voltage changes to 1.2 volts, current equals 1.2 volts divided by 5.0 ohms, which is 0.24 amperes, or 240 milliamperes.",
      "type": "formula"
